      "narration": "A package manager installs software along with everything it depends on, from a curated repository, with cryptographic signatures verifying it came from who it claims. That is a great deal better than downloading a binary from a website and hoping. The commands differ by distribution but the concepts map one to one, so learning one teaches you all of them. The distinction to get right immediately is update versus upgrade, because the names are genuinely misleading. Apt update does not update any software at all — it refreshes the local catalogue of what versions are available. Apt upgrade is what actually installs newer versions. So you always run update first, then upgrade; running upgrade alone works from a stale catalogue and may install nothing or an old version. On Fedora, dnf refreshes automatically, which is arguably a better design. Two lookups worth knowing for when you inherit a machine and find a mystery file: dpkg dash capital S on Debian systems, and rpm dash q f on Red Hat ones, both answer which package installed this file. That is often how you work out what a previous administrator set up and whether you can safely remove it.",
