      "heading": "Why container images go minimal",
      "narration": "This slide is the bridge to your next course, and it explains a practice that looks obsessive until you understand the reasons. Container images are built from a base image, and the sizes vary enormously — a full Ubuntu is around eighty megabytes, Debian slim under thirty, Alpine about seven, and a distroless image around two with no shell in it at all. Why does anyone care? Three reasons. Speed: that image is pulled over the network on every deploy and onto every node, so size multiplies out quickly. Cost: registry storage and bandwidth are real. But the most important is security. Every package installed in that image is code, and code has vulnerabilities. A full distribution image ships hundreds of packages you never use, and each one can generate a security alert you must assess and patch. A minimal image simply does not contain them, so the alerts never arrive. Now the technical detail on this slide that catches everyone. Each Dockerfile instruction creates a layer, and layers are stacked, never modified. So deleting files in a later RUN does not shrink the earlier layer — the data is still in the image, just hidden. That is why the install and the cleanup must be chained into one RUN command.",
